KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17 --object-bits 12
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ conversion op to vector resolves: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
Currently: "found no match for symbol 'C'" resolving the functional
cast to the deduced parameter inside the instantiated operator.
ROUND-96 LOCALIZATION (sharp): the failure belongs to the CONVERSION
OPERATOR path, not to functional casts or deduced parameters:
  * `C tmp; tmp.push_back(*begin()); return tmp;` inside the SAME
    operator works, so C really is std::vector<int> there;
  * `return C();` inside the operator works;
  * the identical cast `return C(begin(), end());` in a plain MEMBER
    TEMPLATE `template <class C> C to() const` on the same class
    VERIFIES (kernel fc6);
  * `C(b, e)` in a free function template `template <class C> C make(...)`
    verifies (fc4), and `std::vector<int> v(arr, arr+3)` verifies (fc3).
Only in the conversion operator does constructor lookup go wrong, and
the candidates reported are `std::allocator`'s constructors -- i.e. the
lookup scopes into the LAST template argument of vector<int,
allocator<int>> instead of vector itself ([class.conv.fct] +
[over.match.ctor]).  A one-line workaround exists in user code (use a
named member template), which is why this layer only surfaces through
libc++/CBMC-style ranges adaptors.
ROUND-96 reduction (cvise with a real g++ -Werror gate): `sum` needs no
BODY -- a declaration suffices, so the failure is purely in converting
`ranget` to `const std::vector<int>&` at the call.  (With no body the
expected verdict is a nondet result, so this test asserts the FRONT END
resolves the conversion: currently it reports "found no match for
symbol 'C'".)  A hand-written vector-like target with default, copy and
SFINAE-free iterator-pair constructors does NOT reproduce (kernel fc11),
so libstdc++'s real vector -- most likely its constrained iterator-pair
constructor -- is required.

ROUND-100 MECHANISM (probe on resolve()'s id_set and the active template
map, inside the instantiated operator): the operator's parameter C is
bound to `std::allocator<signed_int>` in one instantiation and to
`std::initializer_list<signed_int>` in another -- never to
`std::vector<int>`.  Those are vector's CONSTRUCTOR PARAMETER types, so
CBMC is exploring user-defined conversions from `ranget` to each
candidate constructor's parameter ([over.match.copy], [over.best.ics]),
which is legitimate -- and then instantiating the operator's BODY for
those candidates.  `allocator<int>(begin(), end())` has no such
constructor, so the body fails, and that failure escapes as a HARD error
("found no match for symbol 'C'") instead of merely discarding the
candidate.
Per [temp.inst]/1 and [temp.deduct]/8 only the DECLARATION is needed to
decide a conversion candidate; the body must not be instantiated (or its
failure must be soft, as in a SFINAE context).  Two candidate fixes to
try next, in order:
  1. instantiate the conversion operator's body under sfinae_contextt
     while exploring conversion candidates, so a failure discards the
     candidate;
  2. check whether partial ordering is picking the wrong winner before
     instantiation at all (the candidate scan in
     cpp_typecheck_conversions.cpp defers instantiation until a unique
     winner is chosen -- if `allocator` wins, that ordering is the bug).

ROUND-101 ATTEMPT (a), reverted: mark the instance created in
user_defined_conversion_sequence's Phase 3 with
`#conversion_exploration` and, in typecheck_method_bodies, drop such a
body silently when its conversion fails.  Result: the "found no match
for symbol 'C'" errors still appear (so the failing bodies are NOT the
symbols carrying the flag -- the deferred entry is evidently keyed
differently), main is still dropped, AND a new first error appears
(`stl_vector.h:707: symbol 'vector' does not uniquely resolve`), i.e.
nil-ing those bodies perturbs later resolution.  Next steps, in order:
  1. find which symbol the failing deferred body actually belongs to
     (print the symbol name at the failure and compare with the
     instance name from Phase 3) before flagging anything;
  2. then prefer fix candidate (b): stop the losing candidates from
     being instantiated at all -- verify whether partial ordering
     ([over.match.conv], [temp.deduct.conv]) is even reached, since the
     conversions explored here (to allocator, to initializer_list) are
     for DIFFERENT target types and should each be decided
     independently.

ROUND-101 CORRECTION (probe on the deferred-body drain): the operator
bodies are NOT converted in that drain -- the only body failure reported
there is `main` itself.  The `found no match for symbol 'C'` errors carry
the operator's own function context and are emitted while MAIN's body is
being converted, i.e. the instantiated operator's body is converted
INLINE during the conversion exploration, outside Phase 3's
sfinae_contextt, and its errors escape as an ERROR-COUNT increase which
then makes main's conversion fail.
NEXT STEP (well-defined): convert the instantiated conversion operator's
body INSIDE Phase 3's sfinae context (or discard any candidate whose body
conversion raises errors) so a losing candidate cannot poison the
enclosing expression.  That is a slightly larger change than the two
attempts recorded above, which is why it is left for the next round
rather than rushed.

ROUND-102 DIAGNOSIS -- the missing rule is N5008 [over.best.ics]/4.
Corrections to the notes above, both established by rebuilding
build-work with and without a change (the earlier claim compared against
the STALE frozen reference binary and was wrong):
  * suppressing the losing candidate's body failure changes NOTHING here
    (identical error list), so attempt (a) and its variant are no-ops;
  * `symbol 'vector' does not uniquely resolve` (stl_vector.h:707) was
    ALREADY the first error before any change, and is the real
    first-order failure.
That ambiguity lists four vector constructors -- (const allocator&),
(size_t, const allocator&), (const vector&), (initializer_list,
const allocator&) -- all viable for a single `ranget` argument, each via
a user-defined conversion using ranget's `template <class C> operator
C()`.  g++ 13 and clang++ both accept the program silently, because
[over.best.ics]/4 says that when the target is the FIRST PARAMETER of a
constructor and that constructor is a candidate by [over.match.copy]
(copy-initialization of a class -- which is what passing `r` to
`sum(std::vector<int>)` is), USER-DEFINED CONVERSION SEQUENCES ARE NOT
CONSIDERED.  All four constructors are therefore NON-VIABLE, and the only
candidate left is ranget's own conversion function with C deduced as
std::vector<int> ([temp.deduct.conv]).
This one rule explains BOTH layers: it removes the ambiguity AND stops
the bogus C = std::allocator<int> / C = std::initializer_list<int>
explorations at the root, so the losing-candidate body failures never
arise in the first place.
IMPLEMENTATION SKETCH (next round): CBMC has no flag for this -- add a
counter + RAII guard on cpp_typecheckt, set it around the
constructor-candidate resolution reached from
user_defined_conversion_sequence -> new_temporary -> cpp_constructor
(that path IS [over.match.copy]), and honour it in
user_defined_conversion_sequence by refusing a user-defined conversion
for that parameter.  Note the guard must apply to the ctor's first
parameter only, and must NOT leak into nested initializations; also note
DIRECT-initialization (`std::vector<int> v(r)`) does allow user-defined
conversions to constructor parameters, so the guard must not be set on
that path.
